# Copy to .env for local development. This file contains placeholders, never live credentials.
# Production must set NODE_ENV=production and supply its own values through a secret manager.
NODE_ENV=development
SIPPER_BASE_URL=http://localhost:3100
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://sipper:sipper_local_only@127.0.0.1:5547/sipper
# Production: require verified TLS, for example append ?sslmode=verify-full.
# For a private CA, also use &sslrootcert=/run/secrets/database-ca.crt and mount
# that public CA file read-only into every application/migration/worker container.
# The hostname must match the database certificate. Do not use sslmode=no-verify.
# Generate independently: openssl rand -base64 48
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=replace-with-an-independent-random-secret-at-least-32-characters
# Generate: openssl rand -base64 32. Keep old key IDs available when rotating stored secrets.
# JSON object of key ID -> base64-encoded 32-byte AES key. Never commit the populated value.
# Also encrypts per-customer OIDC connection settings. Configure company sign-in
# in the portal, not global environment keys; HTTPS issuer, client credentials,
# redirect URI and verified email-domain DNS are required before enabling it.
SIPPER_ENCRYPTION_KEYS={"v1":"REPLACE_WITH_BASE64_32_BYTE_KEY"}
SIPPER_ENCRYPTION_KEY_ID=v1

# Managed text messaging uses the existing database, identity and encryption keys.
# Apply migrations 032 and 033 before updating the portal and email worker (pnpm worker).
# Apply migration 035 before upgrading email recovery controls or the email worker.
# Owner Service status lists exhausted invitation emails and can queue the original
# valid invitation again. No new key is needed. Queue creation/attempt timestamps
# are recorded; missing legacy attempt times remain unknown. The email worker
# skips unusable invitations and erases expired/revoked/accepted queued payloads
# during its minute cleanup, including exhausted jobs. SMTP acceptance is not
# inbox delivery and an uncertain SMTP response can result in duplicate emails.
# Retained bodies are server-readable AES-GCM ciphertext, not end-to-end encrypted.
# Keep old key IDs until their retained messages and backups no longer need them.
# Days: integer 1..3650. 90 is a development default; confirm the service retention
# policy before launch. Reads immediately hide expired messages; the email worker
# deletes at most 1,000 expired rows per minute. Backups have separate retention.
# Each read snapshot expires within 30 seconds; clients must clear expired history
# even when a conversation-list refresh succeeds. No browser offline cache.
# Groups support 20 people, owner-managed rosters/names and participant leaving.
# New/rejoining people cannot read older messages. Text: 4096 UTF-16 units, 60 sends/minute and
# 30 new conversations/hour per user/customer. No new push provider is needed
# for portal, Mac/iOS, Windows or Android messaging. Apply the API before upgrading clients.
# All four native clients use the same API and have separate opt-in generic chat
# alerts implemented locally. Mobile alerts require migration 034, the push worker,
# each user's opt-in and the provider settings below (normal APNs topic on iOS;
# existing FCM project on Android). Mac/Windows alerts poll while running and need
# no provider key. Real OS/provider delivery remains unverified. No offline archive.
# API, access and release limits: ../docs/MESSAGING.md.
SIPPER_MESSAGE_RETENTION_DAYS=90

# Optional self-hosted managed meetings. Leave all five empty until configuring
# the service; metadata remains available and joins report service unavailable.
# API key/secret match the private LiveKit YAML. Admission secret is a different
# random value shared only with the signalling proxy. Never expose port 7880.
# Combined Compose private origin: http://meetings-media:7880
# Public URL must point to Sipper's signalling proxy, e.g. wss://meet.sipper.dev
SIPPER_MEETING_PUBLIC_URL=
SIPPER_MEETING_INTERNAL_URL=
SIPPER_MEETING_API_KEY=
SIPPER_MEETING_API_SECRET=
SIPPER_MEETING_ADMISSION_KEY=
# Start worker:meeting separately. Its progress becomes stale after 45 seconds.
# It retries real SFU removal/end requests; admission alone is not media proof.

# SCIM has no global provider key. Apply migration 024, then create a customer
# provisioning token in Portal > Customer > More > User provisioning. Copy the
# displayed /api/scim/v2/<customer-id> base URL and one-time bearer token to the
# identity provider. Only its hash is stored; tokens expire after one year and
# rotation immediately invalidates the old token. Do not put tokens in URLs,
# logs, source control or browser storage. Use the public HTTPS SIPPER_BASE_URL
# in production; the existing edge/origin authentication settings still apply.
# Revoking a token stops future sync requests, not previously granted access.
# Full mappings, lifecycle and interoperability checks: ../docs/SCIM.md.

# Local Mailpit (infra/compose.dev.yml). In production configure authenticated SMTP and TLS.
SMTP_HOST=127.0.0.1
SMTP_PORT=1029
SMTP_SECURE=false
SMTP_USER=
SMTP_PASSWORD=
MAIL_FROM=Sipper <accounts@sipper.localhost>

# Stripe: test keys during integration, live keys only after the live-sales checks pass.
# No Stripe key means billing endpoints return a setup-required error; no fake paid entitlements.
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=
# Signing secret from the endpoint/Stripe CLI, distinct from the API key.
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# Recurring per-user prices configured in Stripe. Currency and price live in Stripe.
STRIPE_PRICE_MONTHLY=
STRIPE_PRICE_ANNUAL=
# Local/test-only billing exists only when explicitly enabled outside production.
SIPPER_ALLOW_TEST_BILLING=false

# Native clients are public OAuth clients: they never receive an OAuth client secret.
# These exact callbacks are registered by the migration/bootstrap script.
SIPPER_NATIVE_REDIRECTS=sipper://oauth/callback,com.hybes.sipper:/oauth/callback
# Local Mac Development scheme only: append com.hybes.sipper.development:/oauth/callback,
# then run db:migrate to reseed clients. Do not add development redirects to production.
# Maximum managed-configuration and company-directory cache age; does not promise
# upstream PBX revocation. Directory pages require an active licensed membership
# and enrolled device on every request. Clients must use the earliest page expiry,
# discard a partial snapshot if its revision changes, and clear managed contacts
# on sign-out, withdrawal or expiry. No contacts are merged into personal stores.
# Mac/iOS/Windows/Android directory views and protected caches are implemented.
# Full portal-to-app calling and mobile visual acceptance remain unverified.
# No external directory provider or additional key is required.
SIPPER_CONFIG_TTL_SECONDS=3600
# My devices uses existing verified browser sessions and no new keys/settings.
# Self-service revocation preserves licences and established calls; offline direct
# SIP configuration may remain usable until this TTL or upstream PBX revocation.
# Calling preferences (migration 026) need no new keys. A user's Do Not Disturb
# excludes all their devices from future gateway calls/pushes in that customer.
# Existing recipients, calls, registrations and outgoing calls remain unchanged.
# All four apps expose the preference in managed settings. Updated direct clients
# apply it after configuration refresh (normally each minute), returning 486 for
# new incoming calls. Offline clients keep the last policy until this configuration
# TTL expires. Older apps may ignore it; this does not change the upstream PBX.
# Manual availability (migration 029) also needs no keys or service. Available,
# Away, Busy and Not set belong to the customer membership and persist until the
# user changes them. They do not change calling or prove reachability. Apply the
# migration and deploy the portal before updated clients expose this control.
# Comma-separated literal IPs of reverse proxies directly in front of the portal.
# Empty means use the socket peer; forwarded headers from other peers are ignored.
# Configure your proxy to append/replace X-Forwarded-For with its observed client IP.
# List every trusted hop; never add customer/client ranges. IPv4 and IPv6 accepted.
SIPPER_TRUSTED_PROXY_IPS=
# Production origin authentication: generate an independent random secret (32+ characters),
# and set the same SIPPER_EDGE_SHARED_SECRET using `wrangler secret put` in website/.
# Never ship it in app clients, public config or wrangler.jsonc. Blank is allowed only
# outside production. When set, every origin route except GET/HEAD /api/health requires it.
# The website Worker overwrites forwarding headers and supplies its observed client IP.
# That authenticated IP is used for native telemetry and authentication rate limiting;
# SIPPER_TRUSTED_PROXY_IPS applies only to direct local/development requests without it.
SIPPER_EDGE_SHARED_SECRET=
# Workspace staff, including read-only support, must enrol MFA before management access.
# Do not disable in production.
SIPPER_REQUIRE_ADMIN_MFA=true

# Gateway callback authentication. Blank disables /api/gateway/* with a setup-required error.
# Generate independently: openssl rand -base64 48 (at least 32 characters).
# Give this key only to the SIP gateway, never native apps. Use verified TLS via
# the public Worker or a trusted private ingress (see infra/INGRESS.md).
# This identifies one gateway service; it is not a SIP password or portal auth secret.
# Gateway controllers also supply their generated X-Sipper-Controller instance ID.
# API calls other than heartbeat require that instance's current ownership lease.
SIPPER_GATEWAY_ID=primary
SIPPER_GATEWAY_API_KEY=
# Public TLS SIP hostname and port delivered to managed devices. Blank host keeps
# the authenticated readiness heartbeat disabled. Use a hostname whose certificate
# is trusted by released apps. This is separate from the private gateway API URL.
SIPPER_GATEWAY_PUBLIC_HOST=
SIPPER_GATEWAY_PUBLIC_PORT=5061

# Apple Push Notification service. All four values are required for iOS VoIP delivery.
# Key ID and Team ID are the 10-character identifiers from your Apple developer account.
# PRIVATE_KEY is the .p8 PEM, with literal \n escapes or a quoted multiline env value.
# TOPIC is the signed iOS app bundle ID followed by .voip: com.hybes.sipper.ios.voip
# for Release, com.hybes.sipper.ios.development.voip for Debug.
# Sandbox must match the installed app's aps-environment entitlement. Use false for distribution.
# One topic/environment is supported per deployment. Keep Debug on a separate staging
# portal/database/gateway with SANDBOX=true. Enable SIPPER_PUSH_ENABLED in the signed
# iOS build as documented in ios/.env.example; never send Debug tokens to production.
# No Apple account password, notarisation password or Developer ID certificate is used here.
SIPPER_APNS_KEY_ID=
SIPPER_APNS_TEAM_ID=
SIPPER_APNS_PRIVATE_KEY=
SIPPER_APNS_TOPIC=
# Normal message alerts use the same signing key/environment and a separate iOS
# device token. This is the EXACT app bundle ID WITHOUT .voip:
# Release: com.hybes.sipper.ios; Debug: com.hybes.sipper.ios.development.
# Keep blank to build/run without Apple chat delivery. Never use the VoIP topic
# here. The iOS user must enable Message notifications and grant OS permission.
# Alerts contain only "Sipper / New message" and opaque device/conversation IDs;
# no names, body text, SIP credentials, emails or phone numbers go to APNs/FCM.
# Apply migration 034 before upgrading clients or starting the push worker.
SIPPER_APNS_MESSAGE_TOPIC=

# Mac/Windows message alerts need no push topic or credential. Running clients poll
# their owned-device /messages/alerts endpoint with normal native bearer auth.
# The feed contains unread references, never decrypted bodies. It is capped at
# 100 conversations per customer per scan page and has not been production load-tested.
SIPPER_APNS_SANDBOX=false

# Firebase Cloud Messaging HTTP v1. Server-only JSON from the service account for this app's
# Firebase project. Required fields: project_id, client_email, private_key. Keep JSON on one
# line with escaped newlines inside private_key. Do not commit the populated value or JSON file.
# Enable the FCM API and grant this service account permission to send project messages.
# OAuth uses Google's fixed token endpoint; a token_uri in the JSON cannot change its destination.
SIPPER_FCM_SERVICE_ACCOUNT=
# Must match the installed Android applicationId registered with Firebase.
SIPPER_ANDROID_PACKAGE=com.hybes.sipper.android
# Isolated debug installs use com.hybes.sipper.android.development and a separate
# Firebase Android app; match android/.env.example to the same test project.

# Ended call rows and completed push jobs are removed after this many hours (1..720).
# Caller details are encrypted while ringing and erased on answer/end; push payloads carry
# only opaque references. Chat uses the same worker with a five-minute alert
# deadline, at most four attempts and one collapsed generic notification per app.
# Read messages and withdrawn recipients are skipped before delivery. Provider
# acceptance does not prove display; an in-flight generic alert cannot be recalled.
# The push worker performs expiry/retention cleanup each minute.
# Retention covers incoming/outgoing gateway calls and linked transfer attempts.
# Transfer destinations are encrypted; ordinary call records omit dialled numbers.
# Direct SIP calls are outside this history.
SIPPER_CALL_RETENTION_HOURS=24
# Start pnpm worker:push separately from pnpm worker (the email worker).
# Each process records progress only after a queue check completes, at most every
# 15 seconds. Owner Service status marks push stale after 45 seconds and email
# stale after 180 seconds; polling is not proof of successful provider delivery.
# The application Compose file checks the same progress locally, without a new
# secret or public endpoint. Each container runs one worker and needs its existing
# writable /tmp mount. `node --import tsx scripts/worker-health.ts email` (or push)
# exits nonzero when its progress file is missing, malformed or stale.
# Alert on unhealthy containers using your host monitoring service. No external
# alerts are sent or monitoring account configured by these environment settings.
# Missing provider keys do not produce fake successful notifications. Run and configure
# the SIP gateway as well; these keys alone do not enable incoming calls. Android client
# configuration is in android/.env.example. Verify delivery on locked/idle devices before launch.

# Renewal grace is an explicit commercial policy. Zero pauses new managed access on expiry.
SIPPER_BILLING_GRACE_DAYS=0
# Separate database for automated tests. Name must end with _test. Never use production.
SIPPER_TEST_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://sipper:sipper_local_only@127.0.0.1:5547/sipper_test
# Optional controller-container replacement/combined media-loss rehearsal.
# First build gateway/Dockerfile; this test-only setting uses disposable loopback
# services and a temporary named state volume. See gateway/README.md.
# SIPPER_GATEWAY_TEST_IMAGE=sipper-controller:local
# Optional actual Asterisk/controller Compose deployment rehearsal. Run without
# a native stage; it uses the same fictional fixture and a private test-only relay
# to the built loopback portal. Requires the current Asterisk/controller images.
# SIPPER_GATEWAY_COMPOSE=1
# Optional native stage for `pnpm test:gateway`: enable one platform at a time.
# Mac uses an isolated XCTest store; iOS creates/deletes a disposable simulator.
# Windows runs its real AppState/C engine on macOS with null audio and a temporary test CA.
# This does not exercise Windows Schannel, DPAPI or the packaged app. No host trust changes.
# Requires the native libraries and local gateway prerequisites in gateway/README.md.
# SIPPER_MACOS_GATEWAY=1
# SIPPER_WINDOWS_GATEWAY=1
# SIPPER_IOS_GATEWAY=1
# SIPPER_ANDROID_SERIAL=emulator-5556
# Also used by pnpm test:android-portal. This requires the dedicated SipperUIQA AVD,
# built Android debug/test APKs, pnpm build, local Mailpit (SMTP 1029 / HTTP 8030)
# and a local test database role allowed to create/drop its temporary test database.
# It generates isolated auth/encryption keys; no provider keys or production changes.
# pnpm test:ios-portal uses the same services and database permissions, plus Xcode,
# XcodeGen, built iOS libraries and a simulator named "Sipper iOS QA". It creates
# and deletes a separate simulator, with ad-hoc signing for real Keychain access.
# Its temporary SIPPER_TEST_PORTAL_FILE setting is generated by the harness only.
# pnpm test:windows-portal uses the same services and database permissions, plus
# the built Windows C engine (on macOS). Token storage is an in-memory fixture;
# the harness creates/removes SIPPER_TEST_PORTAL_FILE. No provider keys are needed.
# Windows DPAPI/Schannel, packaged UI and audible media need separate acceptance.
# Optional adb binary override for either Android harness; default is the macOS SDK.
# SIPPER_TEST_ADB=/absolute/path/to/Android/sdk/platform-tools/adb
# Directory status/activity sharing needs migrations 030/031; no new key/service.
# Separate default-off consents per membership; snapshots expire within 30 seconds.
# Managed call observations expire after three minutes and never imply reachability.
